======================================================================
2.7 IDENTIFIERS
======================================================================

An IDENTIFIER is a name given to a program element.

Examples:

    age
    total
    sum
    studentName
    calculateSum


Identifiers can name:

    • Variables
    • Functions
    • Structures
    • Enumerations
    • Labels
    • Other declared entities


Example:

    int age = 25;

Here:

    age
        → identifier


======================================================================
2.8 RULES FOR IDENTIFIERS
======================================================================

Basic rules:

    1. Identifier may contain letters.
    2. Identifier may contain digits.
    3. Underscore (_) is allowed.
    4. Identifier cannot start with a digit.
    5. Spaces are not allowed.
    6. Keywords cannot be used as identifiers.
    7. C identifiers are case-sensitive.


Valid examples:

    age
    total
    student_name
    studentName
    marks1
    _count


Invalid examples:

    1age
        → starts with digit

    student name
        → contains space

    total-marks
        → '-' is not part of an identifier

    int
        → keyword

    my@name
        → '@' is not valid in a standard identifier


======================================================================
2.9 CASE SENSITIVITY
======================================================================

C is case-sensitive.

Therefore:

    age
    Age
    AGE
    aGe

are different identifiers.

Example:

    int age = 20;
    int Age = 30;

These are two different variables.


Similarly:

    main
    Main

are different names.


INTERVIEW POINT:

    C is case-sensitive.


======================================================================
2.10 NAMING CONVENTIONS
======================================================================

Naming conventions are not generally syntax rules.
They are programming style practices.

Poor:

    int x;
    int a;
    int q;

Better:

    int age;
    int totalMarks;
    int studentCount;


Common styles:

    camelCase:
        totalMarks
        studentCount

    snake_case:
        total_marks
        student_count


For job-ready programming:

    Prefer descriptive names.

Instead of:

    int x;

Prefer:

    int studentCount;


Instead of:

    int a;

Prefer:

    int totalMarks;


======================================================================
2.11 CONSTANTS
======================================================================

A constant is a value that does not change during
the relevant execution/context of a program.

Examples:

    10
    3.14
    'A'
    "Hello"


Example:

    int age = 25;


Here:

    25
        → integer constant


Different types of constants/literals include:

    Integer constants
    Floating constants
    Character constants
    String literals


======================================================================
2.12 INTEGER CONSTANTS
======================================================================

Examples:

    10
    25
    -50
    0
    1000


Integer constants can also be written in different bases.

Decimal:

    25


Octal:

    031


Hexadecimal:

    0x19


Binary:

    0b11001

Binary integer constants are supported in newer C standards
such as C23; older C standards do not define 0b literals.


All represent the same numerical value:

    25


======================================================================
2.13 SUFFIXES FOR INTEGER CONSTANTS
======================================================================

Integer constants can use suffixes.

Examples:

    10U
    10L
    10UL
    10LL
    10ULL


Meaning:

    U
        → unsigned

    L
        → long

    LL
        → long long


Example:

    unsigned int x = 100U;

    long y = 100L;

    long long z = 100LL;


======================================================================
2.14 FLOATING CONSTANTS
======================================================================

Examples:

    3.14
    10.5
    0.25
    -5.75


Scientific notation:

    1.5e3

means:

    1.5 × 10^3
    = 1500


Examples:

    2.5e2
        = 250

    3.0e-2
        = 0.03


Suffixes:

    3.14f
        → float literal

    3.14L
        → long double literal


Without a suffix:

    3.14

is normally a double constant.


======================================================================
2.15 CHARACTER CONSTANTS
======================================================================

A character constant is written using single quotes.

Examples:

    'A'
    'B'
    '1'
    '#'
    '\n'


Important:

    'A'
        → character constant

    "A"
        → string literal


They are NOT the same thing.


Example:

    char grade = 'A';


Escape characters can also be used:

    '\n'
    '\t'
    '\\'
    '\''
    '\"'
    '\0'


======================================================================
2.16 STRING LITERALS
======================================================================

A string literal is written inside double quotes.

Examples:

    "Hello"
    "C Programming"
    "M.Tech"
    "12345"


Example:

    printf("Hello World");


Here:

    "Hello World"
        → string literal


A C string is represented as a sequence of characters
terminated by a null character '\0'.


Conceptually:

    "CAT"

is stored as:

    'C' 'A' 'T' '\0'


Strings will be studied in detail in the Strings chapter.


