======================================================================
2.19 STATEMENTS
======================================================================

A statement is an instruction that forms part of a C program.

Example:

    int x = 10;

This is a declaration statement.

Example:

    x = x + 5;

This is an expression statement.


Example:

    return 0;

This is a return statement.


Multiple statements:

    int a = 10;
    int b = 20;
    int sum = a + b;
    printf("%d\n", sum);


Each performs a specific action.


======================================================================
2.20 DECLARATION
======================================================================

A declaration tells the compiler about an identifier
and its type or other properties.

Example:

    int age;


Here:

    int
        → type

    age
        → identifier


Another example:

    int calculateSum(int, int);


This declares a function.


Declaration and definition are related but different concepts.


======================================================================
2.21 INITIALIZATION
======================================================================

Initialization gives an object its initial value
at the time it is created/defined.

Example:

    int age = 25;


Here:

    int
        → type

    age
        → identifier

    =
        → initialization syntax

    25
        → initial value


Another example:

    int x = 10;


======================================================================
2.22 ASSIGNMENT
======================================================================

Assignment changes the stored value of an already existing
modifiable object.

Example:

    int x = 10;

    x = 20;


First:

    x = 10

initializes x.


Then:

    x = 20

assigns a new value to x.


Important distinction:

    Initialization:
        int x = 10;

    Assignment:
        x = 20;


======================================================================
2.23 EXPRESSIONS
======================================================================

An expression is a combination of operands, operators,
function calls, constants, etc., that represents a value
or computation.

Examples:

    10 + 20

    a + b

    x * y

    x > 10

    a = b + c

    functionCall()


Example:

    int sum = a + b;


Expression:

    a + b


It calculates a value that is then used for initialization.


======================================================================
2.24 BLOCKS
======================================================================

A block is a sequence of declarations and statements
enclosed in braces.

Example:

    {
        int x = 10;
        printf("%d", x);
    }


Function body:

    int main(void)
    {
        printf("Hello");
        return 0;
    }


Conditional block:

    if (x > 0)
    {
        printf("Positive");
    }


Blocks create scopes for many declared identifiers.


======================================================================
2.25 WHITE SPACE
======================================================================

Whitespace includes things such as:

    Spaces
    Tabs
    Newlines


Example:

    int x = 10;


and:

    int
    x
    =
    10
    ;


may represent the same tokens, although formatting
strongly affects readability.


Whitespace is often used to separate tokens and improve
human readability.


Good style:

    int totalMarks = 500;


Poor style:

    int totalMarks=500;


Both can be valid, but the first is easier to read.


======================================================================
2.26 INDENTATION
======================================================================

C does not generally use indentation to define blocks.

Braces define blocks.

Example:

    if (age >= 18)
    {
        printf("Adult");
    }


This formatting is recommended because it makes the
structure easy to understand.


Bad formatting:

    if(age>=18){printf("Adult");}


It may still be valid, but it is harder to read.


Job-ready rule:

    Write readable code.


======================================================================
2.27 COMMENTS
======================================================================

Comments are ignored as normal executable instructions.

Two common forms:

    1. Single-line comment
    2. Multi-line comment


Single-line:

    // This is a comment


Multi-line:

    /*
       This is a
       multi-line comment
    */


Example:

    int age = 25;  // Student age


======================================================================
2.28 WHY USE COMMENTS?
======================================================================

Comments can explain:

    • Why something is done
    • Complex logic
    • Assumptions
    • Important constraints
    • TODO items


Good comment:

    // Convert temperature from Celsius to Fahrenheit.


Poor comment:

    // Add 1 to x
    x = x + 1;


The second comment merely repeats obvious code.


JOB-READY RULE:

    Write useful comments, not comments for every line.


