======================================================================
2.35 DECLARATION vs DEFINITION
======================================================================

This distinction becomes important in interviews.


DECLARATION:

    Tells the compiler about an entity.

Example:

    int add(int, int);


DEFINITION:

    Provides the actual implementation/storage,
    depending on the kind of entity.

Function definition:

    int add(int a, int b)
    {
        return a + b;
    }


Variable definition:

    int x = 10;


For job interviews, remember:

    Declaration:
        "This exists and has this type."

    Definition:
        "Here is the actual definition/implementation
         or storage for it."


======================================================================
2.36 HEADER FILES — BASIC INTRODUCTION
======================================================================

A header file typically contains declarations,
macros, type definitions, etc., intended to be shared
across source files.

Examples:

    <stdio.h>
    <stdlib.h>
    <string.h>
    <math.h>


Example:

    #include <stdio.h>


This provides declarations associated with standard
input/output facilities.


User-defined headers can also be included:

    #include "myheader.h"


Difference:

    <header.h>
        → commonly searches implementation/system
          include paths

    "header.h"
        → commonly searches local/source-related paths
          first, then implementation-defined include paths


Exact search rules depend on the implementation.


